1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,225
Welcome to Module Ten, the last module of Unit Three.

2
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:09,137
Touch a hot stove, and your hand is already moving before you feel the pain.

3
00:00:09,137 --> 00:00:14,337
A reflex arc in your spinal cord made that decision in a few thousandths of a second.

4
00:00:14,337 --> 00:00:16,562
Your brain hears about it afterwards.

5
00:00:16,562 --> 00:00:19,200
Nervous systems are layered like that.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,150
Cheap, fast circuits for routine stimuli.

7
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:25,212
Expensive deliberation for everything else.

8
00:00:25,212 --> 00:00:27,887
Right now, your agent has no reflexes.

9
00:00:27,887 --> 00:00:33,437
Every decision, big or small, goes to the same model at the same price.

10
00:00:33,437 --> 00:00:36,450
Today you give it a reflex layer, called a router.

11
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:40,850
Its job is to look at a task and decide which model it actually needs.

12
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:46,900
One team at LangChain did exactly this for their internal coding agent, because their bill kept climbing.

13
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:52,325
First they studied a week of real requests and found that difficulty varied enormously.

14
00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:57,875
Then they picked three models, a fast one, a balanced one, and a strong one.

15
00:00:57,875 --> 00:00:59,675
Then they wrote a short router.

16
00:00:59,675 --> 00:01:09,587
A brief instruction, pick the least expensive model likely to finish the task, plus one line describing each tier, and a small classifier to make the call.

17
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:11,450
Then they tested it properly.

18
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:16,362
Half the work went through the router, and half always went to the strongest model.

19
00:01:16,362 --> 00:01:21,012
The share of work that ended in an accepted change was essentially the same.

20
00:01:21,012 --> 00:01:24,987
But the typical cost per task fell by almost two thirds.

21
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:31,425
And when they tried the opposite, sending everything to the cheapest model, engineers complained within a day.

22
00:01:31,425 --> 00:01:34,175
Routing is not about using the cheap model.

23
00:01:34,175 --> 00:01:36,062
It is about using the right one.

24
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:37,800
Two details matter.

25
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:45,650
The router decides once, at the start of a task, because switching models halfway through throws away the cache you learned about in Unit Two.

26
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:49,225
And the router has to be evaluated, like any other model.

27
00:01:49,225 --> 00:01:54,012
That last point is where some of today's sources are refreshingly honest.

28
00:01:54,012 --> 00:01:57,550
One builder ran a skill router quietly for a week.

29
00:01:57,550 --> 00:02:02,350
Out of more than five hundred suggestions, only about one in twenty was used.

30
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:03,587
He removed it.

31
00:02:03,587 --> 00:02:10,725
Another found that his routed helpers were cheaper than one setting, but more expensive than simply running a single strong agent.

32
00:02:10,725 --> 00:02:12,975
A router is a hypothesis.

33
00:02:12,975 --> 00:02:15,587
You have to test it against the right comparison.

34
00:02:15,587 --> 00:02:18,500
And here is where the metaphor fails.

35
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:25,862
Reflexes were tuned by selection over millions of years, and when a reflex misfires, you usually notice.

36
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:29,950
A router is only as good as the examples and tests behind it.

37
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:34,450
And when it sends a hard task to a weak model, nothing flinches.

38
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:37,700
You just get a plausible, slightly worse answer.

39
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:42,675
In today's lab you will write twenty tasks and label how hard each one is.

40
00:02:42,675 --> 00:02:48,725
You will build two routers, one from simple rules and one using the cheapest model, and score both.

41
00:02:48,725 --> 00:02:53,387
Watch especially for under-routing, where a hard task is sent to a weak model.

42
00:02:53,387 --> 00:02:56,050
Then you will wire the router into your agent.

43
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,675
That completes your Unit Three harness.

44
00:02:58,675 --> 00:03:01,562
Open your terminal, and let's build a reflex.

